<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nùng people</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Nùng are a Tai-speaking people of north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, especially the border provinces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-bang">Cao Bằng</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lang-son">Lạng Sơn</a>, closely related to the Tày and to the Zhuang of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. In the 11th century the Nùng chief Nùng Trí Cao rebelled against both Đại Việt and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> China. In 1947 the French recognised a Nùng autonomous territory around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mong-cai">Móng Cái</a>, and many Nùng moved south after 1954.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nung-people.jpg" alt="Nùng people" width="320" height="702" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: DXLINH · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nung_woman.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Nùng live mainly in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> and south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangxi">Guangxi</a>, and sometimes call themselves Thổ, meaning native to the land. Vietnam's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> counted 968,800 in 2009 and 1,083,298 in 2019, the third largest Tai-speaking group after the Tày and the Thái. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> they are classed with the Tày as Zhuang, and many scholars of the Tai peoples consider the Zhuang and Nùng a single people known by different names in the two countries. The historian Keith Taylor calls the Vietnamese terms categories of French colonial knowledge.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Under the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song dynasty</a> the Nong clan rose to rule an area called Temo, reaching south to Guangyuanzhou, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-bang">Cao Bằng</a> province. Its leader Nùng Trí Cao, known in Chinese as Nong Zhigao, founded a Kingdom of the Great Succession in 1042, was captured by Đại Cồ Việt and released, then proclaimed the Kingdom of the Southern Heavens in 1048. He was defeated by the Song in 1055, and many rebels fled into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> around Cao Bằng and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lang-son">Lạng Sơn</a>. There hereditary thổ ty officials governed the frontier, paying tribute to both Chinese and Vietnamese courts, and most Nùng communities ruled themselves until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viet-minh">Viet Minh</a> took the Việt Bắc region in 1953.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-yat-sen">Sun Yat-sen</a> recruited fighters from Nùng villages on the Vietnamese side of the border, and the French of the Tonkin Protectorate saw Nùng strength as a threat while also courting them. After 1975, as tension grew with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a> doubted the loyalty of the Nùng. The Viet Bac Autonomous Zone was revoked, schooling was conducted in Vietnamese, and in the 1980s an estimated 250,000 ethnic Vietnamese were settled along the Chinese border.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Nùng farm terraced hillsides, growing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/water-wheel">water wheels</a> for irrigation, along with vegetables, corn, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peanut">peanuts</a>, tangerines, persimmons, anise and bamboo. Like the Tày they are known for silversmithing, weaving, papermaking and embroidery, often in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indigo">indigo</a>. Society is patrilineal and grouped in clans named after the regions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> they came from. Their language was written in Sawndip, a script based on Chinese characters. Many follow an indigenous religion led by village shamans, influenced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucianism">Confucianism</a>, and worship Quan Âm and their ancestors.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nùng people” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Nung</b> and Tai areas of the north were also attached.</blockquote><a href="/state/crown-domain-of-the-nguyen-dynasty">Crown Domain of the Nguyen Dynasty</a></li><li><blockquote>The Mac ruled a small upland territory of Tay and <b>Nung</b> peoples from Cao Bang, relying on diplomatic protection from the Ming and then the Qing.</blockquote><a href="/state/mac-rule-in-cao-bang">Mac rule in Cao Bang</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1947 French authorities, seeking allies against the Viet Minh, recognised an autonomous territory in the Hai Ninh area around Mong Cai for the <b>Nung</b>, as the largely Chinese-speaking border communities there were known.</blockquote><a href="/state/nung-autonomous-territory">Nung Autonomous Territory</a></li></ul></section>
